  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Over the 26 days since v48.0 (September 3), US forces struck IRGC targets inside Iran on September 1 and Iran retaliated with ballistic missiles the same day; Kuwait's air defenses engaged Iranian missiles on September 2. September 9 was the largest single tanker-destruction day of the conflict: CENTCOM confirmed five Iranian crude carriers struck in the Strait of Hormuz. From approximately September 10 a de facto operational pause took hold — 21 consecutive days without announced US offensive strikes as of September 29. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>At the UN General Assembly on September 22–23, Iranian FM Araghchi and US envoys Witkoff and Kushner convened a ~3-hour session with Qatari Prime Minister Al Thani as on-site mediator — the first physically co-located Iran-US engagement since June. Iran delivered a written 60-day ceasefire road map to the US delegation on September 23–24; SNSC Secretary Rezaei attached a '4–5 day' deadline (expiring approximately September 28–29). The session fell five days past F3's September 17 horizon. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>Trump publicly rejected what he characterized as Iran's '7-day Hormuz plan' on September 26; National Security Advisor Waltz described it as 'a pretty cynical attempt.' Araghchi responded on September 27 (NBC interview) that Waltz 'has not read' the proposal, describing it as covering a ceasefire, phased Hormuz reopening, and a structured talks timeline — broader than the Hormuz-only piece Trump rejected publicly. Brent crude rose +2.3% on the news. On September 29, Araghchi met Qatari mediators; US diplomatic sources quoted by India TV/ANI expected a US reply 'hopefully today' or by Monday. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F11</span></p>

      <p>Channel 12 (September 23) published a poll showing Yashar 23 seats, Likud 20, with Eisenkot's coalition-eligible bloc at approximately 54 seats versus Netanyahu's ~50. An earlier Channel 12 poll put Yashar at 25, Likud at 21. An outlier from i24NEWS (September 23) reversed the order at Likud 27, Yashar 22. Haaretz described Likud as 'gaining ground' from an August low of 19 to ~20–21 while still trailing Yashar in four of five polls. The Knesset dissolved on July 17 at mandatory term end with the coalition holding 62 seats — never having dropped below the 61-seat majority threshold before dissolution. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:35%;">Escalation 35%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:48%;">Protracted 48%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:17%;">De-esc 17%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (35%):</strong> F8 resolves NO (Trump rejects the full road map as he did the Hormuz-only piece), the operational pause collapses, and IRGC mine activity or a new tanker strike cycle restarts before late October.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (48%):</strong> Shuttle diplomacy continues indefinitely without a convened session (F9 resolves NO); Hormuz stays at &lt;5 transits/day (F11 resolves NO); both Israeli blocs fight to a near-tie (F5 and F10 remain unresolved until Oct 27).</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (17%):</strong> F8 and F9 both resolve YES — a formal US response and a convened session by Oct 13 produce a phased deal framework, war-risk premiums drop, and Hormuz commercial traffic begins recovery toward F11's 10/day threshold.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Press TV's September 23–24 coverage of the UNGA contacts presented the Araghchi–Witkoff session as a diplomatic achievement within the framework of Iran's 'resistance economy,' emphasizing that Iran 'presented a plan' rather than framing Iran as the party seeking talks. Araghchi's NBC interview on September 27 — in which he stated Iran was 'ready for a doomsday war' but pursuing diplomacy — was carried by state media with the war-readiness framing, not the diplomatic-overture framing. The NBC interview served as a dual-track domestic message: hardliners received 'doomsday war' while the international audience received 'pursuing diplomacy.' → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>IranWire reported on September 24 that hardliner factions within the IRGC hierarchy expressed open opposition to the Araghchi–Witkoff UNGA session, characterizing it as a strategic concession ahead of the US election. Mojtaba Khamenei — increasingly influential as the Supreme Leader's son and whose public statements have tracked IRGC positioning — remained silent on the UNGA session across all monitored state-media channels through September 29. Kayhan editorial commentary (September 25) did not endorse the 60-day road map and published a piece titled 'America's track record justifies no confidence.' → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>State-media framing of the Strait of Hormuz has consistently presented any closure as a sovereign defensive measure rather than a commercial disruption, treating the September 20 single-transit day as unreported or framed in the passive voice ('limited commercial traffic continued'). The UNGA speech by Araghchi focused on 'resistance economy' as a permanent framework, not a temporary response — a framing that would politically complicate any state-media reversal on Hormuz access. → supports <span class="cite">F11</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

